    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>A 1031 exchange lets a Texas investor sell a rental or other investment property and roll the proceeds into new investment real estate without paying federal tax on the gain at the time of sale. To get the full deferral, you must use a qualified intermediary, identify replacement property within 45 days, close within 180 days, and reinvest all of your net proceeds into property of equal or greater value.</p>
<p>Texas has no personal income tax, so for most individual investors a 1031 exchange in Texas is purely a federal tax play. Here is how the rules work, where people get tripped up, and a worked example. Exchanges have no do-overs, so plan every one with a CPA or tax attorney.</p>

<h2>What qualifies: real property only, held for investment</h2>
<p>Since the Tax Cuts and Jobs Act took effect in 2018, section 1031 applies only to real property. Equipment, vehicles and other personal property no longer qualify. The IRS instructions for Form 8824 limit like-kind treatment to real property held for use in a trade or business or for investment, other than property held primarily for sale. The 2025 federal tax law left section 1031 unchanged.</p>
<p>"Like-kind" is broad. Investment real estate in the United States is generally like-kind to other U.S. investment real estate, regardless of type or quality:</p>
<ul>
<li>A single-family rental in Katy for a fourplex in Spring Branch</li>
<li>Raw land in Waller County for a strip center</li>
<li>A duplex for a fractional interest in a Delaware Statutory Trust</li>
</ul>
<p>What does not qualify:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Your primary residence.</strong> It may qualify for the section 121 exclusion instead, covered in our guide to <a href="/blog/capital-gains-tax-on-home-sale/">capital gains tax on a home sale</a>.</li>
<li><strong>Flips and inventory.</strong> Property bought primarily to resell, such as a fix-and-flip, is held for sale, not investment.</li>
<li><strong>Foreign real estate</strong> exchanged for U.S. real estate, or the reverse.</li>
</ul>
<p>The same taxpayer that sells must buy. If an LLC owns the relinquished property, the same LLC (or the same tax owner, for a single-member LLC) generally needs to take title to the replacement.</p>

<h2>The two deadlines: 45 days and 180 days</h2>
<p>Both clocks start the day you close on the sale of your relinquished property, and they run at the same time.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>45-day identification period.</strong> By midnight of day 45, you must identify potential replacement properties in a signed written document delivered to your qualified intermediary or another permitted party, with a clear description such as a street address or legal description.</li>
<li><strong>180-day exchange period.</strong> You must close on the replacement property by the earlier of day 180 or the due date (including extensions) of your tax return for the year of the sale. A sale late in the year can run into the April filing deadline, so you may need to file an extension.</li>
</ul>
<p>Neither deadline is extended for weekends or holidays. Relief generally comes only from IRS postponements after federally declared disasters, which Texas investors have seen after hurricanes and other declared disasters.</p>

<h2>Identification rules: 3-property, 200% and 95%</h2>
<p>The Treasury regulations limit how many properties you can name. You must satisfy one of these tests:</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>What you can identify</th><th>Catch</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>3-property rule</td><td>Up to three properties of any value</td><td>The most common choice; simple and flexible</td></tr>
<tr><td>200% rule</td><td>Any number of properties</td><td>Combined value cannot exceed 200% of the value of what you sold</td></tr>
<tr><td>95% exception</td><td>Any number, any value</td><td>You must actually acquire at least 95% of the total value identified</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>Identify backups. Inspections fail, appraisals come in low and sellers back out. Naming three properties when you plan to buy one is cheap insurance.</p>

<h2>The qualified intermediary and why you never touch the money</h2>
<p>If the sale proceeds land in your account, even briefly, the exchange is generally blown. In a standard delayed exchange, a qualified intermediary (QI) signs an exchange agreement with you before the sale closes, receives the proceeds from the title company, and uses them to buy the replacement property on your behalf.</p>
<p>Your agent, attorney, accountant or anyone who has worked for you in those roles in the prior two years generally cannot serve as your QI. Ask how the QI holds funds, whether accounts are segregated, and whether it carries a fidelity bond and errors-and-omissions coverage. If a QI fails or mishandles funds, your exchange can fail with it, so treat choosing one as seriously as choosing a lender.</p>

<h2>Boot: what triggers tax in an exchange</h2>
<p>Anything you receive that is not like-kind property is boot, and it is taxable up to the amount of your realized gain. The two common forms:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Cash boot:</strong> proceeds you keep, or proceeds used for non-closing items such as loan points or prorated rent credits.</li>
<li><strong>Mortgage boot:</strong> paying off a larger loan than you take on, without adding cash to cover the difference.</li>
</ul>
<p>The practical rule for full deferral: buy at least as much as you sold (net of selling costs), reinvest all of the net equity, and replace the debt you paid off with new debt or added cash.</p>

<h2>Depreciation recapture: the hidden reason to exchange</h2>
<p>Rental owners deduct depreciation every year, and when they sell, that depreciation comes back as taxable gain. For residential and commercial buildings, the portion of gain from prior straight-line depreciation is "unrecaptured section 1250 gain," taxed at a federal rate of up to 25%, with the remaining long-term gain taxed at capital gains rates. High earners may also owe the 3.8% net investment income tax.</p>
<p>A properly structured 1031 exchange defers all of it. Your old basis carries over into the new property, so the deferred gain is still there, but you keep the full equity working. Investors who keep exchanging until death may leave property to heirs, who under current law generally receive a stepped-up basis.</p>

<h2>Worked example: selling a Houston rental</h2>
<p>A hypothetical investor bought a rental for $250,000 years ago, took $60,000 of depreciation, and now sells for $400,000 with $24,000 of selling costs. The figures are illustrative, not a forecast, and ignore state tax because Texas has none for individuals.</p>
<table>
<thead><tr><th>Line</th><th>Sell and pay tax</th><th>1031 exchange</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>Sale price</td><td>$400,000</td><td>$400,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Selling costs</td><td>$24,000</td><td>$24,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Adjusted basis ($250,000 minus $60,000)</td><td>$190,000</td><td>$190,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Realized gain</td><td>$186,000</td><td>$186,000</td></tr>
<tr><td>Unrecaptured 1250 gain (up to 25%)</td><td>$60,000, taxed now</td><td>Deferred</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remaining capital gain (assume 15%)</td><td>$126,000, taxed now</td><td>Deferred</td></tr>
<tr><td>Estimated federal tax, before NIIT</td><td>Up to about $33,900</td><td>$0 if fully reinvested</td></tr>
<tr><td>Basis in replacement property</td><td>Full purchase price</td><td>Carryover basis, adjusted for any added cash or debt</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
<p>With the exchange, the investor buys a $450,000 replacement and puts every dollar of net equity into it, keeping roughly $33,900 more working than in the taxable sale. If the investor instead pocketed $30,000 at closing, that $30,000 would be boot and taxed. Model the replacement property in our <a href="/tools/rental-property-calculator/">rental property calculator</a> and your sale in the <a href="/tools/seller-net-proceeds-calculator/">seller net proceeds calculator</a> before you list.</p>

<h2>Reverse and improvement exchanges</h2>
<p>A <strong>reverse exchange</strong> lets you buy the replacement first. Because you cannot own both properties in the exchange at once, an exchange accommodation titleholder holds title to one of them under IRS Revenue Procedure 2000-37. The same 45-day and 180-day limits apply, measured from when the titleholder takes title, and you need cash or financing to buy before you sell. These cost more than standard exchanges.</p>
<p>An <strong>improvement (build-to-suit) exchange</strong> uses the same parking structure so the titleholder can fund renovations or new construction with exchange money. Only improvements completed and in place by the time you take title within the 180 days count toward the value you reinvest.</p>

<h2>Delaware Statutory Trusts and Texas investors</h2>
<p>A Delaware Statutory Trust (DST) is a trust that owns institutional real estate, such as apartment complexes or warehouses, and sells fractional interests. Under IRS Revenue Ruling 2004-86, a properly structured DST interest can count as replacement real property. DSTs appeal to landlords tired of tenants and repairs or who need a fast backup identification. The trade-offs: you give up control, interests are generally illiquid, and they are securities sold with fees, so read the offering documents and talk to an independent advisor.</p>

<h2>Common 1031 mistakes Texas investors make</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Calling the QI after closing.</strong> The exchange agreement must be in place before the sale closes and before any proceeds are paid out. Tell your title company at the start that the sale is part of an exchange.</li>
<li><strong>Vague identification.</strong> "A rental in Cypress" is not enough. Use the street address or legal description, and deliver the signed list on time.</li>
<li><strong>Buying down in value.</strong> Trading a $400,000 property for a $300,000 one leaves taxable boot, even if every dollar of equity goes into the new deal.</li>
<li><strong>Selling to or buying from relatives.</strong> Exchanges with related parties come with a two-year holding requirement and extra scrutiny under section 1031(f).</li>
<li><strong>Converting the new rental into a home too soon.</strong> Moving into the replacement property shortly after the exchange invites questions about whether you really held it for investment. Ask your CPA how long to rent it first.</li>
<li><strong>Forgetting Form 8824.</strong> You report the exchange on IRS Form 8824 with your return for the year you sold.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Bottom line</h2>
<p>A 1031 exchange in Texas can defer federal capital gains tax and depreciation recapture, and the state adds no income tax of its own. Set up a qualified intermediary before closing, line up backups before day 45, reinvest everything and replace the debt. Browse more strategies in our <a href="/category/investing/">investing guides</a>.</p>
